[{:db/id "q-26594/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer/id #uuid "ad368c5b-0cb2-529b-9e46-903b3993c33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" :answer/id #uuid "153c2ce8-7eb9-5641-b916-f32801aad129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594/selection/0b1431686812a0dd4c4eeec6eb87a803e4a7d04ff802c2260e99693426195d70" :answer/id #uuid "6ad6f9c9-32b7-5ae8-beed-184a772b2483" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde" :answer/id #uuid "6fb90d16-e7d0-5e52-a9fd-df72d647e0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594/selection/bbfd2b07786245819e2e91f26a4ef8e027e05def7f6b7b047ffd4f9538889061" :answer/id #uuid "2984184b-7a52-589b-81d1-99ec20577215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "71cc3638-abd0-5b43-9039-38f6aabc33fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-26594/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer-field/choices ["q-26594/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" "q-26594/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" "q-26594/selection/0b1431686812a0dd4c4eeec6eb87a803e4a7d04ff802c2260e99693426195d70" "q-26594/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde" "q-26594/selection/bbfd2b07786245819e2e91f26a4ef8e027e05def7f6b7b047ffd4f9538889061"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26594" :question/id #uuid "d6d26fbc-87de-5a39-ae14-d0903e431edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-26594/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26594"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-26398"] :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection/3afe31c6e2870e08f1c6deac3efb90ebef9ea8d09cbaca15893b7457808c020d" :answer/id #uuid "9a2b9367-f612-5594-9d46-96eb84706463" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection/c1d8471ae41991574263b6d6523b52fda51d01f60a102f0ae39b2aed49a002ef" :answer/id #uuid "81404138-8b74-582d-9845-2ed3426cfc3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-2,b+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection/3d5e26bf9ad74383ec90958f3f3b4600274f2756a2703fc5443e718280e883a6" :answer/id #uuid "c805b4ba-f771-515a-be67-bf766c50f3df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection/3197bb9e00150f618747cc5f448603ab4147de857c6124017ccefcac97315672" :answer/id #uuid "9fa1dedf-3384-504c-b5fe-b7c935c4e2f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-2,3b)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection/fd843367747156efa398d2099a08ce200a238ef0b577ccd66d3f0502afb8f6a6" :answer/id #uuid "44e8a603-073c-50fe-9ac8-b87d0b1dc921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a+2,b-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3efcfaf-b884-53eb-acac-188ddab361b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121383/selection/3afe31c6e2870e08f1c6deac3efb90ebef9ea8d09cbaca15893b7457808c020d" :answer-field/choices ["q-121383/selection/3afe31c6e2870e08f1c6deac3efb90ebef9ea8d09cbaca15893b7457808c020d" "q-121383/selection/c1d8471ae41991574263b6d6523b52fda51d01f60a102f0ae39b2aed49a002ef" "q-121383/selection/3d5e26bf9ad74383ec90958f3f3b4600274f2756a2703fc5443e718280e883a6" "q-121383/selection/3197bb9e00150f618747cc5f448603ab4147de857c6124017ccefcac97315672" "q-121383/selection/fd843367747156efa398d2099a08ce200a238ef0b577ccd66d3f0502afb8f6a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121383" :question/id #uuid "a10acc39-43de-5cce-97e4-fe2c174ea53c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121383" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-121383/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121383"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121220"] :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection/814d6694b9247b59ccea16a6aa57ee3d6286a70fbe21d12ed69660edcf81bbda" :answer/id #uuid "444d2778-897f-5555-9dab-2143abff26f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a+3,b-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection/d21b6b1de780644960697d018f064d97d44a69086ff3d78b24cb9d2427f5add6" :answer/id #uuid "49f12b0b-5f0f-5dff-8eae-9267bf4e0189" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a+3,b+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection/3d5e26bf9ad74383ec90958f3f3b4600274f2756a2703fc5443e718280e883a6" :answer/id #uuid "83ba7556-a7ad-5384-bd3b-a2bfb2a130c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection/c156d3f0a71712dae4c60f3244213a08f76b860b1850d3caec66b8f2aba9a800" :answer/id #uuid "f0a35399-d2a8-5a0d-8719-f2a8c25d53ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection/420736995881d543e0036af971bebdd08b1ebd601e16f95ab7447d4660acb057" :answer/id #uuid "0f37a632-fcb0-5eb7-8179-bb757432b129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(a-3,b+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e37858ef-144f-5790-8662-8e48d881ca8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121385/selection/c156d3f0a71712dae4c60f3244213a08f76b860b1850d3caec66b8f2aba9a800" :answer-field/choices ["q-121385/selection/814d6694b9247b59ccea16a6aa57ee3d6286a70fbe21d12ed69660edcf81bbda" "q-121385/selection/d21b6b1de780644960697d018f064d97d44a69086ff3d78b24cb9d2427f5add6" "q-121385/selection/3d5e26bf9ad74383ec90958f3f3b4600274f2756a2703fc5443e718280e883a6" "q-121385/selection/c156d3f0a71712dae4c60f3244213a08f76b860b1850d3caec66b8f2aba9a800" "q-121385/selection/420736995881d543e0036af971bebdd08b1ebd601e16f95ab7447d4660acb057"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121385" :question/id #uuid "92febfde-8323-5fd1-8145-c17f29e2f7b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-121385" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-121385/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection/4ec4dbda2081a25eb39e5c54be41fffc44e2d0516a4b93b95619181850a8f9ad" :answer/id #uuid "499b9f48-7c20-5f5d-95f0-7450b1e2ede8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection/2f640d3e9c34ec4171f3b948ab19c01be7220087f7745dd0b7846f8ab146829f" :answer/id #uuid "4a3e38f7-870d-5c43-b308-779f761d21ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection/c458adc0a35928794f8ba37832a700126e1bb5546ab8e5f5d8e2f6ba12dae01b" :answer/id #uuid "cd83fa09-2618-55b4-abc6-34d8e3d3d4f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection/f1c72963f33c0862d48b8a0d3db37a9a15f0086f42a830087c9caa8a9b6c8a48" :answer/id #uuid "3bc867c5-1527-58a5-9aaf-23cc044c851f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection/360fca4011015cc49cdadf6055c61b28b61a631cfedf9ec7e4105462f0fe99b7" :answer/id #uuid "dcdaa654-e1cb-5608-bd8a-9b8eebb79e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eec2f6d6-894c-565c-8778-6ba3d564b96d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121418/selection/4ec4dbda2081a25eb39e5c54be41fffc44e2d0516a4b93b95619181850a8f9ad" :answer-field/choices ["q-121418/selection/4ec4dbda2081a25eb39e5c54be41fffc44e2d0516a4b93b95619181850a8f9ad" "q-121418/selection/2f640d3e9c34ec4171f3b948ab19c01be7220087f7745dd0b7846f8ab146829f" "q-121418/selection/c458adc0a35928794f8ba37832a700126e1bb5546ab8e5f5d8e2f6ba12dae01b" "q-121418/selection/f1c72963f33c0862d48b8a0d3db37a9a15f0086f42a830087c9caa8a9b6c8a48" "q-121418/selection/360fca4011015cc49cdadf6055c61b28b61a631cfedf9ec7e4105462f0fe99b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121418" :question/id #uuid "670d2002-d2ca-56f3-b34d-cba198a70401" :question/math-academy-id "q-121418" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-121418/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121418"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" :answer/id #uuid "22a1fc25-0f47-5da2-88fe-7848ed0cfa4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection/298289ff2783728b9c035911ec6fe053a7e35464abb98323c2f17c9d57e49b1f" :answer/id #uuid "2b8d5f9a-e300-55ce-b35a-88a9c86329ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection/a217012f5a27b7915ceffd419057c4616817727e56168e68517d35ec9e175893" :answer/id #uuid "0eeb552e-db1d-5d57-b573-db3e004a76cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c" :answer/id #uuid "3a080da0-b380-5a19-9121-b2d59105962c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection/662c277f350ac1a8ffcfa5efcf73a8a540e2cf3e387c66c7db66386198588dd2" :answer/id #uuid "9ff28aad-c29c-515c-afb4-4e1238ca2bd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb8f0439-1450-54f7-b298-b79bbb50d05a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121414/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c" :answer-field/choices ["q-121414/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" "q-121414/selection/298289ff2783728b9c035911ec6fe053a7e35464abb98323c2f17c9d57e49b1f" "q-121414/selection/a217012f5a27b7915ceffd419057c4616817727e56168e68517d35ec9e175893" "q-121414/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c" "q-121414/selection/662c277f350ac1a8ffcfa5efcf73a8a540e2cf3e387c66c7db66386198588dd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121414" :question/id #uuid "54460c74-a647-5bf9-b4a5-f9969098c52c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-121414/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121414"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection/c6a1f0b35fb86f12a0e1138b6e2c0461f265df1c7e058c326ad8966bfd682852" :answer/id #uuid "1a28a9bf-81d2-5aaf-9099-161905647890" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection/9227689a53ffed4a0bb68e6ec54ed701bb29f9a8d0acfc86ca1343251d753879" :answer/id #uuid "39fbd5b7-b979-53ef-b7e5-ff2bc4aa874d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" :answer/id #uuid "953cd5c3-97fa-5808-abf1-c0c3752d07f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" :answer/id #uuid "a47ef372-6be6-558e-aa9b-b4fef2da5b7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" :answer/id #uuid "0130bb54-b057-5ca9-839e-a81783dbb72d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6687de-2238-5f37-9e6f-0763d20353bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121407/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" :answer-field/choices ["q-121407/selection/c6a1f0b35fb86f12a0e1138b6e2c0461f265df1c7e058c326ad8966bfd682852" "q-121407/selection/9227689a53ffed4a0bb68e6ec54ed701bb29f9a8d0acfc86ca1343251d753879" "q-121407/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" "q-121407/selection/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" "q-121407/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121407" :question/id #uuid "b5f1d3b8-1a03-52fb-a558-210e8579cf5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121407" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point  $(-3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121407/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121407"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" :answer/id #uuid "b158e99c-91f5-504e-8d31-837ded092ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection/80fdd79931a564cbe8cda0116a1645a581b63abe3cc6ee3b38561998c052cbda" :answer/id #uuid "4779b50b-0ef2-5bc2-865a-ce8fcf519744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection/981a2951e3fccc9ba81c501537ab818060069591c658c07fba6936fa5fd0dd27" :answer/id #uuid "0f7aa2b2-7116-59cc-a55d-6a6257726630" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer/id #uuid "3aedfbff-de2f-5a2b-a4d4-d1c3278fd732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde" :answer/id #uuid "8bbcd33a-e392-52d2-bc13-c126c751c9f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e82234cd-b2c2-5bfe-9450-e9c9beee279c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-26596/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer-field/choices ["q-26596/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" "q-26596/selection/80fdd79931a564cbe8cda0116a1645a581b63abe3cc6ee3b38561998c052cbda" "q-26596/selection/981a2951e3fccc9ba81c501537ab818060069591c658c07fba6936fa5fd0dd27" "q-26596/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" "q-26596/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26596" :question/id #uuid "a08d6bcc-6c8d-59c1-9671-aa0f95bb9926" :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-26596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" :answer/id #uuid "401014e4-b5bb-53dd-984e-927695931a6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection/4118bc58c734397ed6279f4651e412efc9d9063789f27921dfdcc1f70042efb2" :answer/id #uuid "018bc939-0d73-5794-b54c-fe49e335c40c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection/2ab0846437ac577c7ad5a341365117bba109724295c61b8542adab253399be53" :answer/id #uuid "c99c320f-2a35-5a04-ad64-030678d28fec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection/cc0e4f4e8d080594555a574b002679d62af8dcf6204de501ba97ce5b2e6759ab" :answer/id #uuid "cbade78c-41b7-5009-a7f2-dea33ed72507" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection/abc4e01626eb87b45206b4cbe5fadc3db03bb1c6e9e7425dea4cb5a219c87fae" :answer/id #uuid "4f511992-425d-5e2c-9118-8629084264e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7755b91-5d25-5b7f-baeb-83497373fe06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-26408/selection/abc4e01626eb87b45206b4cbe5fadc3db03bb1c6e9e7425dea4cb5a219c87fae" :answer-field/choices ["q-26408/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" "q-26408/selection/4118bc58c734397ed6279f4651e412efc9d9063789f27921dfdcc1f70042efb2" "q-26408/selection/2ab0846437ac577c7ad5a341365117bba109724295c61b8542adab253399be53" "q-26408/selection/cc0e4f4e8d080594555a574b002679d62af8dcf6204de501ba97ce5b2e6759ab" "q-26408/selection/abc4e01626eb87b45206b4cbe5fadc3db03bb1c6e9e7425dea4cb5a219c87fae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26408" :question/id #uuid "1a300bbe-a008-544f-8314-4d9f7fffaeff" :question/math-academy-id "q-26408" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-26408/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26408"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121221"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection/c17bd8292ac252ea374f7a6dd61910ef9eb4e35709f2284ca9c73a043ca8da8b" :answer/id #uuid "32119ca0-32e7-5b86-8475-0881f2ce12b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-7,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" :answer/id #uuid "ab1c7863-f5a1-5b43-b8a5-2b3089fc0aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer/id #uuid "f53e0abc-1eef-5a15-b22e-fddd77bdf41c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection/bcf0e01aebad49ee47918670c04d6527a2d57a4c7bf1856194e89b47ac319c57" :answer/id #uuid "182ddc9d-315c-5e41-b8af-a8c7ad6a8c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection/3d957fb68ef8c1656bc8f708031cfbc2f29fae35bc74c711cd2be3fec5a9be7e" :answer/id #uuid "a37d56e3-e043-5def-ad13-7aa9ca5c8b14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5c6c5c-44d3-5c10-8f9d-791c7d973deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-211288/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer-field/choices ["q-211288/selection/c17bd8292ac252ea374f7a6dd61910ef9eb4e35709f2284ca9c73a043ca8da8b" "q-211288/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" "q-211288/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" "q-211288/selection/bcf0e01aebad49ee47918670c04d6527a2d57a4c7bf1856194e89b47ac319c57" "q-211288/selection/3d957fb68ef8c1656bc8f708031cfbc2f29fae35bc74c711cd2be3fec5a9be7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211288" :question/id #uuid "0fbc72ae-1559-5655-8419-8ec96a874c40" :question/math-academy-id "q-211288" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-211288/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211288"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-186866/selection/b26faf9d4f4f53d00fdab8f304b542e1d6aeae55f15ad082ae446919822c9b84" :answer/id #uuid "3f110463-1eba-550f-af19-ac4156f681f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-186866/selection/a86ef5cadbb83091a3d9d5049709282f2f33d8957c2c130e61fd5bfb96d3535a" :answer/id #uuid "1fbd0137-7466-5492-854b-a7318f6f0341" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186057409 :answer-field/choices ["q-186866/selection/b26faf9d4f4f53d00fdab8f304b542e1d6aeae55f15ad082ae446919822c9b84" "q-186866/selection/a86ef5cadbb83091a3d9d5049709282f2f33d8957c2c130e61fd5bfb96d3535a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-186866"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection/e5c1f515261f1785c4411bf39e18fd362d120089b2df8fd0f85e3f972f37bf07" :answer/id #uuid "09625741-65f2-5df7-b273-6dd5704cef21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" :answer/id #uuid "9309cc78-3269-5a16-94cf-ecd12d4c78c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection/ff992f3bfbf9256bb8a93ba4418b1d3fc4bf7775122685039de83403d09a1dca" :answer/id #uuid "a0d4a6fe-6f58-5aa5-b259-a7015844b6a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection/dba8047b9b21d0edd9e340c2c54e10a470ed337e9ade0ddb0627b6082199dce0" :answer/id #uuid "1d93006c-a8c9-5ec2-98c3-0d5dff192810" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection/d64fb1d86ad534b738c55c0cdd7711503752307f7a8376f7004b159563e09c3f" :answer/id #uuid "133ca763-df80-5101-b301-dfb6ebc1ac45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f02a04b-2be1-53ab-bbb4-250a2c621376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-211303/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" :answer-field/choices ["q-211303/selection/e5c1f515261f1785c4411bf39e18fd362d120089b2df8fd0f85e3f972f37bf07" "q-211303/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" "q-211303/selection/ff992f3bfbf9256bb8a93ba4418b1d3fc4bf7775122685039de83403d09a1dca" "q-211303/selection/dba8047b9b21d0edd9e340c2c54e10a470ed337e9ade0ddb0627b6082199dce0" "q-211303/selection/d64fb1d86ad534b738c55c0cdd7711503752307f7a8376f7004b159563e09c3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211303" :question/id #uuid "9f4525c5-5e4e-5b55-8b15-c9fe5125a6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-211303" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-211303/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211303"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10" :answer/id #uuid "2206ebab-a00d-5166-9813-50c7bbb5a8f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" :answer/id #uuid "1b8b085c-adbe-5ddf-aa9e-307d6a3e5152" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection/7145b5f9400d33e4c7ece3b318fdfe6ed5e4a24a119c374361dafaf59f061a1e" :answer/id #uuid "3a241e67-b5fb-5239-9bed-c11ce05a60f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection/aca3fb0d9ddb49350c290ac929c3cc1d4fe3619668acb3d7b8d6d89671359f83" :answer/id #uuid "578ed652-b7a7-50fc-b2a7-aec34ebfe02c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection/a3d744913ae4bfb601820b33be3599518ee9c77618305b8fab3d1921fe8e1f34" :answer/id #uuid "5fe19725-c4d0-5d75-906f-685d14540edd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b84059e6-392d-5ff7-b216-2452474ee3bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-211466/selection/7145b5f9400d33e4c7ece3b318fdfe6ed5e4a24a119c374361dafaf59f061a1e" :answer-field/choices ["q-211466/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10" "q-211466/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" "q-211466/selection/7145b5f9400d33e4c7ece3b318fdfe6ed5e4a24a119c374361dafaf59f061a1e" "q-211466/selection/aca3fb0d9ddb49350c290ac929c3cc1d4fe3619668acb3d7b8d6d89671359f83" "q-211466/selection/a3d744913ae4bfb601820b33be3599518ee9c77618305b8fab3d1921fe8e1f34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211466" :question/id #uuid "cb74a779-9886-5633-be8a-07053e04402f" :question/math-academy-id "q-211466" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/2d046da9a89fe09f09dbd5e0658422ab8ccee8db680a2955ebe1ff0f361b5c35.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of \n$y=-4f(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-211466/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211466"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
